When you check in a bag, it gets a paper tag with a [[@barcodes|barcode]] and a ten-digit number. The bag then drops onto a conveyor belt and vanishes. Behind the walls is a maze of belts, sometimes kilometres long, where scanners read each tag and push bags left or right towards the right flight.

Every bag is also X-rayed for safety. At the end, handlers load the bags into containers or straight into the plane's belly. At your destination the process runs backwards, ending on the carousel where you wait and hope. Bags that need to change planes are pulled out and sent on again, sometimes with less than an hour to make the connection. A missing bag can usually be traced, because its tag has been scanned at every step.

{{story:barcodes}}

India is one of the world's largest salt makers, and roughly three-quarters of its salt comes from Gujarat. Much of it is made along the coast. In [[@earth-salt-flats|the Little Rann of Kutch]], salt workers called Agariyas use a different method. They pump salty water from under the ground into pans in the flat, baking desert and spend months tending them.

The work is hard. Temperatures can pass 45 °C, [[@how-sunglasses|the glare]] off the white salt is fierce, and many families live in simple huts at the pans for most of the year. Tamil Nadu, especially around Thoothukudi, is India's other big salt producer. Farther north, the Great Rann dries into a [[@gujarat-rann|white desert of natural salt]] that stretches to the horizon.

{{story:salt-march}}

Your [[@eyes-see|eyes]] use two kinds of light-sensing cells: cones for colour and detail, and rods for dim light. Night animals have far more rods. Many also have the tapetum lucidum, a shiny layer behind the retina. Light that slips past the rods hits the mirror and bounces back through them again.

That is why the eyes of cats, dogs, deer, cows and crocodiles glow in headlights. The colour of the glow can be green, yellow, blue or red, depending on the animal. People have no tapetum, so our eyes don't shine back, though a camera flash can make them look red.

{{story:bats-fly}}

No muscle can contract fast enough to throw a punch like this. So the mantis shrimp cheats. A part of its arm shaped like a horse's saddle works as a [[@springs|spring]]. Muscles bend it slowly, storing energy. A tiny latch holds the arm locked.

When the latch releases, the spring snaps back and flings the club forward in less than three thousandths of a second. The club accelerates more than 10,000 times faster than an object falling under [[gravity|gravity]]. A bullet leaving a gun barrel accelerates even faster, but the shrimp's club does it underwater, where moving fast is much harder.

{{story:power-pistol-shrimp}}

Riders feel very different at different points of a loop. At the bottom, the track curves upward sharply while you are moving fastest. It has to push you up hard, so you feel three to five times heavier than normal. Your cheeks sag and it is hard to lift your arms.

At the top, you feel light, sometimes almost weightless, because gravity is doing much of the work of curving you. Those changes in felt weight are called g-forces. Fighter pilots and astronauts feel even stronger ones, and train in giant spinning machines to handle them.

{{story:astronaut-training}}

Some designs we use every day are surprisingly old. The QWERTY layout on keyboards comes from typewriters made in the United States in the 1870s. One popular explanation is that it spread out common letter pairs so the metal arms inside would not jam. Even now, phones and laptops use it, simply because so many people learned it. You can read more in [[@typewriters-keyboards|Why are keyboard letters in such a strange order?]].

Other everyday objects, like the [[@zippers|zip]], took many inventors and many years to get right.

{{story:zippers}}

[[@van-gogh|Vincent van Gogh]] painted *The Starry Night* in 1889, while staying at a hospital in Saint-Rémy, in the south of France. It shows the view from his window, with an added village and a dark cypress tree, under a sky of glowing stars and rolling swirls.

Van Gogh sold very few paintings during his life and died poor the following year. His sad story, and his letters to his brother Theo, made people look again at his work after his death. *The Starry Night* now hangs in the Museum of [[@modern-art|Modern Art]] in New York.

{{story:photography-history}}

Before the piano, the most common keyboard was the harpsichord. Its keys plucked the strings with small hooks, so every note came out at about the same loudness however hard you pressed.

Around 1700, in Florence, an instrument maker named Bartolomeo Cristofori built a keyboard that hit the strings with hammers instead. Press gently and you got a soft note. Press hard and you got a loud one. He called it, in Italian, a "harpsichord with soft and loud". People shortened it to *pianoforte*, then *piano*. It was born in the same Italian cities that had led the [[@renaissance|Renaissance]].

{{story:lever-lift}}

The finest Indian cloth was muslin, especially from around Dhaka, in what is now [[@bangladesh|Bangladesh]]. The best muslins were so thin and light that they seemed almost see-through. Some had poetic names that meant "running water" or "evening dew".

Ancient Romans bought so much Indian cloth, [[@cardamom-pepper|spice]] and jewellery that the writer Pliny the Elder complained, nearly 2,000 years ago, that Rome's gold was flowing east to pay for it. Much later, Mughal emperors and their courts wore Indian silks and muslins, and gave them as precious gifts.

{{story:words-borrowed}}

"Buy one, get one free" is the same as 50% off each item, but only if you wanted two in the first place. If you needed just one, you paid full price and got something extra you might not use.

"Buy two, get one free" works out at a third off each item. "Up to 70% off" means at least one thing in the shop is 70% off. Most things may be 10% or 20% off. "Free gift worth ₹500" tells you what the shop says the gift is worth, which may not be what anyone would pay for it.

{{story:advertising}}

Voices can be copied too. A program listens to recordings of a person and learns how their voice rises and falls, how they pronounce sounds, and how they pause. Then it can read out any text in that voice.

Criminals have used this to phone people pretending to be a boss, or a family member in trouble, asking for money urgently. The FBI in the USA and police in India have both warned about such scams.

{{story:special-effects}}

Sikkim is tiny, with only about six lakh people at the 2011 [[census|census]]. Its farms are mostly small plots on steep terraces. Many farmers there had never used much chemical fertiliser in the first place, especially compared with the plains of Punjab during the [[@green-revolution|Green Revolution]]. That made the switch easier than it would be in a big farming state.

The land is also fragile. Heavy chemical use can wash off slopes into streams and lakes. Sikkim is home to forests of rhododendrons and orchids, the [[@red-panda|red panda]], which is its state animal, and snow leopards. Keeping chemicals out was seen as a way of protecting all of them.

{{story:earthworms}}

Oak galls are round, nut-like lumps that grow on oak trees when certain wasps lay their eggs in the buds. Galls are full of a substance called tannin. When crushed galls are mixed with iron salts, a dark ink forms that soaks into paper or parchment.

This iron gall ink was used in Europe for well over 1,000 years. [[@leonardo|Leonardo da Vinci]] and Johann Sebastian Bach wrote with it. Many old documents in the [[@libraries|Library of Congress]] are written in it. Over time it can eat into paper, so some old [[manuscript|manuscripts]] now have holes where the letters were. Libraries use special methods to protect them.

{{story:vote-how}}

Many factories bake in a tunnel oven, which can be as long as a cricket pitch. The tins creep through it for about 20 to 30 minutes. Heat sets the bubbles in place and turns the outside brown, the same chemistry described in [[@baking|baking]].

The bread comes out far too hot to slice. So it rides on a cooling spiral, a tall winding track where it slowly drops to room temperature for an hour or more. Slicing warm bread would squash it flat. Along the way, metal detectors and cameras check every loaf. A loaf that is too pale, too small or lopsided gets pushed off the line, and the rejects are often turned into breadcrumbs or animal feed rather than thrown away.

{{story:industrial-revolution}}
